<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Piyale Pasha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1515–<a href="/years/1578">1578</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Piyale Pasha was an Ottoman admiral (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapudan-pasha">Kapudan Pasha</a>) under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-ii">Selim II</a>, of Croatian or Hungarian origin. He defeated a Spanish-led fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djerba">Djerba</a> in 1560, commanded the fleet at the unsuccessful siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> in 1565, and took Chios from the Genoese without a fight in 1566. He married a daughter of Selim II.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/piyale-pasha.jpg" alt="Piyale Pasha" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: VikiPicture · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piyale_Pasha_bust_at_Istanbul_Naval_Museum.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Piyale Pasha was Ottoman Grand Admiral, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapudan-pasha">Kapudan Pasha</a>, between 1553 and 1567 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> after 1568. His place of birth is unknown, though he was probably born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. He was of Hungarian or Croatian origin, and one account makes him the son of a shoemaker from Tolna. He is said to have become a soldier and to have been captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in Hungary, perhaps at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a> in 1526. He was educated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enderun-school">Enderun School</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, graduated with the title of Kapıcıbaşı and was appointed Sanjak Bey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He became admiral-in-chief of the Ottoman fleet at the age of 39. In 1554, with a fleet that included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turgut-reis">Turgut Reis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salah-rais">Salih Reis</a>, he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>. In 1555 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> sent him to help <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> at the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-de-medici">Catherine de' Medici</a>, and the fleet repulsed a Spanish attack near Piombino. In 1558 he and Turgut Reis raided the coasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and then Spain, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a>. Philip II then organised a Holy League of Spain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, the Papal States, Savoy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights of Malta</a>, whose fleet under Giovanni Andrea Doria took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djerba">Djerba</a> on 12 March 1560.</p><p>Piyale Pasha arrived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djerba">Djerba</a> on 11 May 1560 with 86 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> and galliots and destroyed the Christian fleet in a matter of hours. He and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turgut-reis">Turgut Reis</a> forced the garrison to surrender and took 5,000 prisoners, including Álvaro de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sande-society">Sande</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. In 1562 he married Gevherhan Sultan, a daughter of the future Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-ii">Selim II</a>. In 1565 he, Kızılahmedli Mustafa Pasha and Turgut Reis failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> against the resistance of the Knights, and Turgut Reis died there. In 1566 he captured Chios, ending the Genoese presence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. In 1568 he became the first Ottoman admiral to reach the rank of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1570 and 1571 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, and Piyale Pasha led the fleet that carried the invasion force. After the Ottoman defeat at Lepanto in 1571 he was called back to command the navy, which was rebuilt to its former size in less than a year. His last naval expedition, in 1573, was a landing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>. He died on 21 January 1578 and was buried at the Piyale Pasha Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mimar-sinan">Mimar Sinan</a> built for him in his final years. He had two sons and three daughters, and several warships of the Turkish Navy have been named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Piali Pasha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1566 the Ottoman admiral <b>Piyale Pasha</b> occupied Chios without a fight, ending Genoese rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/maona-of-chios">Maona of Chios</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peljesac">Pelješac</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.93,17.4&amp;year=1555" aria-label="Pelješac on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4970 639 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Piyale Pasha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5420 909h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5420 909h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.93,17.4&amp;year=1555">What was here in 1555? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
